=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
No fault is present in the network at time of investigation. PC4 (172.16.10.4/24) and Laptop0 (172.16.20.10/24) reside on separate VLANs — VLAN 10 and VLAN 20 respectively — both accessed through switch1. The "box that joins the two halves" the user refers to is router0, which performs inter-VLAN routing in a router-on-a-stick configuration: its sub-interfaces eth0.10 (172.16.10.1/24) and eth0.20 (172.16.20.1/24) serve as the default gateways for each VLAN. VLAN-tagged trunks run switch1 → mswitch1 → router0 on both VLAN 10 and VLAN 20, with all sub-interfaces up and all L2 bridge ports in the forwarding state. MAC forwarding databases on both switch1 and mswitch1 correctly track PC4's and Laptop0's addresses through their respective VLAN 20 and VLAN 10 paths. Bidirectional ping between PC4 and Laptop0 shows 0% packet loss and a clean 2-hop traceroute through router0. Router0 carries no iptables rules that could block traffic. All STP ports are in forwarding state and no loops exist. The most probable cause of the original complaint is a transient STP convergence delay: Linux bridges run standard 802.1Q STP with a 30-second listening-plus-learning period after any restart or link event, during which no data frames forward. Once that window passed, connectivity was fully restored. No corrective action is needed; if the 30-second startup blackout is unacceptable, disabling STP (brctl stp br_vlanX off) on all access-only bridges — which have no redundant paths — would eliminate the delay entirely.
=== END ===